tau = 4 s (dashed)."},{"id":"step-resp-r4-q2","round":4,"type":"mc","prompt":"Preview of second-order systems. A system obeys \\(\\ddot y+2\\dot y+26y=26x\\), whose characteristic roots are \\(s=-1\\pm j5\\). What does its unit-step response look like?","options":["It rises smoothly to 1 with no overshoot, like a first-order system","It oscillates forever with constant amplitude around 1","It settles at 26, the coefficient of x","It rises past 1, rings around 1 with a period of about \\(2\\pi/5\\approx1.26\\ \\mathrm{s}\\), and the ringing dies out with time constant about 1 s"],"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Complex roots \\(-\\sigma\\pm j\\omega_d\\) give a term \\(e^{-\\sigma t}\\cos(\\omega_dt+\\phi)\\): the imaginary part 5 rad/s sets the ringing period \\(2\\pi/5\\), and the real part 1 sets the decay \\(e^{-t}\\). The DC gain is \\(26/26=1\\), so it settles at 1.\n• A smooth rise needs real roots.\n• Constant-amplitude oscillation needs roots on the imaginary axis (zero real part).\n• The final value is the ratio of the constant terms, \\(26/26\\), not 26.","preview":["second-order-sys"],"figure":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 340 210\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Step response of the second-order system: overshoot above 1, then decaying oscillation settling at 1.\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.6\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" font-size=\"12\" style=\"max-width:340px;width:100%;height:auto\"><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"330\" y2=\"176\"/><polygon points=\"330,176 323.6,178.7 323.6,173.3\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"176\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"16\"/><polygon points=\"50,16 52.7,22.4 47.3,22.4\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\"/><text x=\"330\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">t (s)</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"12\" text-anchor=\"start\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\">y(t)</text><polyline po